1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,676
Each dot is one student: hours of study across, marks up.

2
00:00:04,676 --> 00:00:08,647
The dots climb from lower left to upper right, in a tight band.

3
00:00:08,647 --> 00:00:11,590
Study more, score more, on the whole.

4
00:00:11,590 --> 00:00:16,672
One number, r, measures how tightly two quantities move together.

5
00:00:16,900 --> 00:00:19,323
Loosen the band and r falls.

6
00:00:19,323 --> 00:00:23,646
A shapeless blob: r near zero, no relationship.

7
00:00:23,646 --> 00:00:31,316
Tilt it the other way, like price against quantity sold, and r turns negative, down to minus one for a perfect falling line.

8
00:00:31,533 --> 00:00:36,144
Correlation tells you how strongly two things move together.

9
00:00:36,144 --> 00:00:41,181
Regression goes one step further and gives you a line, so you can predict one from the other.

10
00:00:46,667 --> 00:00:51,670
Welcome to Class 12 Mathematics, Unit 10: Correlation and Regression.

11
00:00:54,067 --> 00:00:55,319
Five parts.

12
00:00:55,319 --> 00:00:57,727
Correlation and the scatter diagram.

13
00:00:57,727 --> 00:01:01,321
Karl Pearson's coefficient, r.

14
00:01:01,321 --> 00:01:04,668
The properties of r. Spearman's rank correlation.

15
00:01:04,668 --> 00:01:07,265
And the two regression lines, with predictions.

16
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:18,112
Plot every pair as a dot and the scatter diagram tells you the story before any arithmetic.

17
00:01:18,112 --> 00:01:22,448
Dots on one rising line: perfect positive correlation.

18
00:01:22,448 --> 00:01:25,234
On a falling line: perfect negative.

19
00:01:25,234 --> 00:01:28,490
A tight rising band: strong positive.

20
00:01:28,490 --> 00:01:31,406
A loose falling cloud: weak negative.

21
00:01:31,406 --> 00:01:33,240
A shapeless blob: none.

22
00:01:33,467 --> 00:01:39,784
And a warning: this last cloud clearly follows a curve, yet r comes out near zero.

23
00:01:39,784 --> 00:01:43,711
Karl Pearson's r measures only straight line relationships.

24
00:01:44,933 --> 00:01:46,185
A real example.

25
00:01:46,185 --> 00:01:49,948
The price of tomatoes on five days, and the quantity sold.

26
00:01:49,948 --> 00:01:56,614
Plot the five points: as the price goes up, the quantity comes down, almost along a straight line.

27
00:01:56,614 --> 00:01:59,883
Strong, negative, linear correlation.

28
00:01:59,883 --> 00:02:02,634
We will calculate it exactly in a moment.

29
00:02:07,433 --> 00:02:09,166
Where does the formula come from?

30
00:02:09,166 --> 00:02:12,773
Draw lines through the mean point, x bar, y bar.

31
00:02:12,773 --> 00:02:15,150
They cut the plane into four quarters.

32
00:02:15,367 --> 00:02:20,928
For each point, measure its deviations from the means, d x and d y.

33
00:02:20,928 --> 00:02:27,947
In the top right and bottom left quarters, both deviations have the same sign, so their product is positive.

34
00:02:27,947 --> 00:02:30,408
In the other two, it is negative.

35
00:02:30,408 --> 00:02:34,749
Add up all the products: mostly positive points give a big positive total.

36
00:02:34,967 --> 00:02:44,995
Divide by the square root of sum d x squared times sum d y squared, and the answer is pinned between minus one and plus one, with no units.

37
00:02:44,995 --> 00:02:48,020
That is Karl Pearson's coefficient of correlation.

38
00:02:49,233 --> 00:02:54,235
Reading the value: the sign gives the direction, the size gives the strength.

39
00:02:54,235 --> 00:03:01,435
Above three quarters is high, a half to three quarters moderate, below a quarter practically nothing.

40
00:03:01,435 --> 00:03:05,691
And r squared tells you the fraction of the variation in y that x explains.

41
00:03:06,900 --> 00:03:08,295
Now the tomatoes.

42
00:03:08,295 --> 00:03:14,766
The means are a hundred and sixty and eight, whole numbers, so use deviations from the actual means.

43
00:03:14,766 --> 00:03:20,235
Build the table: d x, d y, their product, and their squares.

44
00:03:20,235 --> 00:03:24,436
The two deviation columns must add to zero, which is your check.

45
00:03:24,667 --> 00:03:31,322
Then r is minus two sixty over root of four thousand times twenty: minus point nine two.

46
00:03:31,322 --> 00:03:34,358
A strong negative correlation, just as the picture said.

47
00:03:35,567 --> 00:03:38,196
When the numbers are small, use the raw-score form.

48
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:43,373
Total the five columns: x, y, x y, x squared, y squared.

49
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:45,285
Put them into the raw-score formula.

50
00:03:45,500 --> 00:03:49,382
Five thirteen over five forty: point nine five.

51
00:03:49,382 --> 00:03:55,583
Very high positive, and r squared says ninety per cent of the variation in marks goes with study hours.

52
00:03:58,200 --> 00:03:59,504
Exam tip.

53
00:03:59,504 --> 00:04:02,616
Whole-number means: use deviations.

54
00:04:02,616 --> 00:04:05,663
Small raw numbers: use the raw-score form.

55
00:04:05,663 --> 00:04:09,087
Awkward means: deviations from an assumed mean.

56
00:04:09,087 --> 00:04:12,675
And set out the table neatly, because the marks are in the columns.

57
00:04:17,033 --> 00:04:18,480
A powerful property.

58
00:04:18,480 --> 00:04:25,094
Slide all the x values along, or stretch them, and the shape of the cloud does not change.

59
00:04:25,094 --> 00:04:25,920
Neither does r.

60
00:04:26,133 --> 00:04:35,366
So you may subtract a convenient number from every x and divide by a common factor, and do the same to y, as long as the factors are positive.

61
00:04:35,366 --> 00:04:37,228
r stays exactly the same.

62
00:04:37,228 --> 00:04:40,396
That is why we can code awkward data before calculating.

63
00:04:41,600 --> 00:04:42,826
Two cautions.

64
00:04:42,826 --> 00:04:47,422
Zero correlation only means no straight line relationship.

65
00:04:47,422 --> 00:04:54,224
And correlation is not causation: two things can rise together because a third thing drives them both.

66
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:05,981
Some things can only be ranked, not measured: dancers placed by judges, students in order of merit.

67
00:05:05,981 --> 00:05:09,080
Spearman's coefficient works with ranks.

68
00:05:09,080 --> 00:05:18,897
Find D, the difference in ranks for each item, and r s is one minus six sum D squared, over n cubed minus n.

69
00:05:18,897 --> 00:05:24,231
For ties, give each the average rank, and add a small correction for each tied group.

70
00:05:25,433 --> 00:05:28,104
Two judges rank eight dancers.

71
00:05:28,104 --> 00:05:33,156
Subtract the ranks for each dancer, square, and add: forty two.

72
00:05:33,156 --> 00:05:40,643
With n equal to eight, r s is one minus two fifty two over five oh four: exactly a half.

73
00:05:40,643 --> 00:05:46,767
Moderate agreement: both judges put B first, but they disagree sharply about E and F.

74
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:48,504
Two more.

75
00:05:49,233 --> 00:05:55,476
With no ties: one minus two sixty four over nine ninety: point seven three three.

76
00:05:55,700 --> 00:06:00,780
With ties, each group of two tied values adds half to the sum of D squared.

77
00:06:00,780 --> 00:06:03,285
Three such groups add one and a half.

78
00:06:03,500 --> 00:06:09,606
So r s is one minus two forty three over nine ninety: point seven five five.

79
00:06:11,633 --> 00:06:15,502
Rank both columns the same way round, highest first.

80
00:06:15,502 --> 00:06:18,333
Mix the directions and the sign of your answer flips.

81
00:06:23,133 --> 00:06:26,181
Rainfall and wheat yield on six plots.

82
00:06:26,181 --> 00:06:28,317
Which straight line fits best?

83
00:06:28,317 --> 00:06:32,288
Pivot a line about the mean point and look at the vertical misses.

84
00:06:32,288 --> 00:06:35,270
Square each miss: the red squares.

85
00:06:35,270 --> 00:06:40,205
The best line is the one that makes their total area as small as possible.

86
00:06:40,205 --> 00:06:41,804
The method of least squares.

87
00:06:42,033 --> 00:06:44,782
The minimum comes at slope point seven two.

88
00:06:44,782 --> 00:06:51,045
Calculus gives the formula: b y x is sum d x d y over sum d x squared.

89
00:06:51,045 --> 00:06:54,417
And the best line always passes through the mean point.

90
00:06:54,417 --> 00:06:57,072
This is the regression line of y on x.

91
00:06:58,300 --> 00:07:00,190
There are two regression lines.

92
00:07:00,190 --> 00:07:05,164
Y on x, for predicting y, minimises the vertical misses.

93
00:07:05,164 --> 00:07:10,385
X on y, for predicting x, minimises the horizontal misses.

94
00:07:10,385 --> 00:07:15,619
Their slopes are the two regression coefficients, and their product is r squared.

95
00:07:15,619 --> 00:07:17,262
Both lines cross at the mean point.

96
00:07:18,467 --> 00:07:19,136
The whole job.

97
00:07:19,700 --> 00:07:25,438
Means twenty five and forty; the table gives thirty six, fifty and thirty two.

98
00:07:25,667 --> 00:07:35,026
So y on x is y equals point seven two x plus twenty two, and x on y is x equals one point one two five y minus twenty.

99
00:07:35,233 --> 00:07:40,266
To predict yield from rainfall, use y on x: forty one point four four.

100
00:07:40,467 --> 00:07:46,192
And r is the square root of the product of the b's: point nine, positive like both b's.

101
00:07:48,200 --> 00:07:49,291
Working backwards.

102
00:07:49,500 --> 00:07:54,199
Both lines pass through the means, so solve them: thirteen and seventeen.

103
00:07:54,400 --> 00:08:01,304
Guess which line is which: the first as y on x gives point eight; the second as x on y gives point four five.

104
00:08:01,533 --> 00:08:07,928
Their product, point three six, is at most one, so the guess was right: r is point six.

105
00:08:07,928 --> 00:08:10,035
A product above one would mean swap them.

106
00:08:10,267 --> 00:08:16,788
Finally b y x equals r sigma y over sigma x gives sigma y equals four.

107
00:08:18,800 --> 00:08:29,231
The classic trap: to estimate y, use the line of y on x; to estimate x, use x on y. Rearranging the wrong one gets no marks.

108
00:08:29,231 --> 00:08:34,992
And a line fitted on twenty to thirty centimetres of rain says nothing reliable about sixty.

109
00:08:37,400 --> 00:08:38,170
Pause and think.

110
00:08:45,467 --> 00:08:51,614
Point six: the square root of point eight times point four five, positive like the b's.

111
00:08:53,600 --> 00:08:54,035
One more.

112
00:09:01,667 --> 00:09:08,700
Strong negative: the size, point nine two, is above three quarters, and the sign is negative.

113
00:09:10,867 --> 00:09:11,975
Quick recap.

114
00:09:11,975 --> 00:09:14,293
Look at the scatter diagram first.

115
00:09:14,293 --> 00:09:22,509
Karl Pearson's r, between minus one and one, measures straight line correlation and ignores changes of origin and scale.

116
00:09:22,509 --> 00:09:25,335
Spearman's formula works with ranks.

117
00:09:25,335 --> 00:09:33,175
The two regression lines meet at the means, their slopes multiply to r squared, and you predict y with the line of y on x.

118
00:09:37,367 --> 00:09:39,699
That was correlation and regression.

119
00:09:39,699 --> 00:09:45,038
Next, probability: conditional probability, independence and Bayes' theorem.

120
00:09:45,038 --> 00:09:45,584
See you there.

